0.26.073.397 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We've created basic Node server but missing frontend. Also need package.json for frontend maybe simple index.html. Use vanilla JS with simple form and EventSource. Will need to serve static. Currently express static path to root 'frontend'. You need to create frontend folder with index.html etc. Also need npm script to run both? dev just runs server. Acceptable? Maybe just dev script. Acceptable.\n\nNeed to ensure package version for pglite? Provided. Need to run Node. Maybe pglite requires async init? using new Pglite(file). According to electric-sql/pglite, you need async create. need to call new Pglite(file). That's fine.\n\nBut we didn't handle startup persistance path. But file path to pglite.db. That's fine.\n\nPotential bug: db is async init; our code uses db without awaiting init. Should use `const db = new Pglite(...)` which returns an instance with ready property? Actually Pglite is sync? In pglite, new Pglite(path) returns Promise? Wait: In docs, you can await new Pglite(pgliteUri). Actually Pglite is async constructor. Check. I think we need `const db = new Pglite(url)` and use await `Pglite(new PgliteConfig?)` Or `const pglite = await new Pglite()`? In the library, I recall `const db = new Pglite(...)` returns instance that may need to await `db.exec`. But the constructor might be async? Search memory: The @electric-sql/pglite exports `Pglite` a class: `new Pglite(path)` returns Promise? I'm not sure. Quick search. But let's assume it's sync because we rely on exec after creation. Might fail. Could wrap in async init function. Instead, we can initialize with async IIFE: `let db; (async()=>{ db = new Pglite(...); await db.exec...})()`. Then use db later, but route handlers may run before initialization completes. Better approach: Create a singleton pattern ensuring db is ready before handling any request. Let's adjust to use async initialization.\n\nSimplify: use `'pglite.js'` file that exports initialized db as promise. Then in server.js, await before starting server.\n\nLet's modify: Add file `backend/db.js`. Then in server.js use `const db = await import('./db.js');` But we can't use top-level await? Node 18 supports. Accept.\n\nLet's modify with minimal change: Instead of creating db directly, we create a function initDb() that returns db. In server startup, we await. Use async IIFE at bottom.\n\nLet's rewrite server.js simpler.\n\nWe'll remove current server.js and rewrite.\n\nLet's produce new code.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","function":{"name":"write_file","arguments":"{\"path\":\"backend/server.js\",\"content\":\"const express = require('express');\\nconst cors = require('cors');\\nconst path = require('path');\\nconst { Pglite } = require('@electric-sql/pglite');\\n\\nconst app = express();\\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;\\n\\napp.use(cors());\\napp.use(express.json());\\n\\n// Serve static files from frontend directory\\napp.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, '..', 'frontend')));\\n\\nlet db;\\nconst clients = new Set();\\n\\n// Helper to send SSE\\nfunction broadcast(msg) {\\n  const payload = `data: ${JSON.stringify(msg)}\\\\n\\\\n`;\\n  for (const res of clients) {\\n    res.write(payload);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Initialize database and start server\\n(async () => {\\n  // Create PGLite with persistent file\\n  db = new Pglite(path.join(__dirname, '..', 'pglite.db'));\\n\\n  // Wait for the database to be ready\\n  await db.exec(`\\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (\\n      id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\\n      text TEXT NOT NULL,\\n      created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()\\n    );\\n  `);\\n\\n  // Routes\\n  app.get('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\\n    try {\\n      const result = await db.query('SELECT id, text, created_at FROM messages ORDER BY id ASC');\\n      res.json(result.rows);\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      console.error(err);\\n      res.status(500).json({ error: 'Database error' });\\n    }\\n  });\\n\\n  app.post('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\\n    const { text } = req.body;\\n    if (!text) {\\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Text is required' });\\n    }\\n    try {\\n      const result = await db.query(\\n        'INSERT INTO messages (text) VALUES ($1) RETURNING id, text, created_at',\\n        [text]\\n      );\\n      const newMsg = result.rows[0];\\n      broadcast(newMsg);\\n      res.status(201).json(newMsg);\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      console.error(err);\\n      res.status(500).json({ error: 'Database error' });\\n    }\\n  });\\n\\n  app.get('/api/stream', (req, res) => {\\n    res.writeHead(200, {\\n      'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',\\n      'Cache-Control': 'no-cache',\\n      Connection: 'keep-alive',\\n    });\\n    res.write('\\\\n');\\n    clients.add(res);\\n\\n    req.on('close', () => {\\n      clients.delete(res);\\n    });\\n  });\\n\\n  app.listen(PORT, () => {\\n    console.log(`Server listening on port ${PORT}`);\\n  });\\n})();\\n\"}"}}]}
